 145: ## 75. The fast outcome: exact reserve exclusion, no conversion
 146: 
 147: ### 75.1 A count-to-deficit census on the terminal approach
 148: 
 149: For either F role `P`, every P-unblocked six-window contains at most all
 150: physical P stones.  Hence
 151: 
 152: `mu_P >= 6-|X_P|`.                                          (75.1)
 153: 
 154: This elementary lower bound is enough to audit every reserve checkpoint.
 155: 
 156: **Theorem S64 (fast terminal-approach reserve census) [PROVEN].**  Fix a pure
 157: `sigma`, a reachable S15 synchronization `h`, and a genuine one-for-one
 158: post-S15 F continuation with no unmatched real-F placement.  Suppose `z_4`
 159: is `Fhat`'s sixth physical stone and wins.  Then the four pre-query states
 160: have the following exact count/reserve data:
 161: 
 162: | state | `(|X_Fhat|,|X_F|)` before query | minimum information | `RES_1` |
 163: |---|---:|---|---|
 164: | `q_1` | `(2,1)` | `mu_H=4`, `mu_R>=5` | fails |
 165: | `q_2` | `(3,2)` | `mu_H=3`, `mu_R>=4` | fails |
 166: | `q_3` | `(4,3)` | `mu_H=2`, `mu_R>=3` | fails |
 167: | `q_4` | `(5,4)` | `mu_H=1`, `mu_R>=2` | fails |
 168: 
 169: Moreover, `z_3` hits a shadow deficit-two window at `q_3` and forces the
 170: terminal-proximate transition
 171: 
 172: `(2,mu_R>=3) -> (1,mu_R'>=2)`                               (75.2)
 173: 
 174: before the mandatory query of `z_4`.  If `mu_R=3` at `q_3`, this is exactly
 175: S58's `(2,3)` first-event cliff.  If `mu_R>3`, S55 says that scalar
 176: `F-CAD_2^st` is already absent at `q_3`.
 177: 
 178: *Proof.*  S15 has F-role counts shadow/real `2/1`; each genuine paired F
 179: event adds one to each.  Equation (75.1) gives the first two rows and the
 180: real lower bounds in the last two rows.
 181: 
 182: Let `V` be a winning six-window completed by `z_4`.  At that event `Fhat`
 183: has exactly six stones total, so all six lie in `V`.  At each `q_j`, exactly
 184: the `j+1` already-present `Fhat` stones lie in `V`, and the later
 185: prescriptions are its remaining holes.  No `Shat` stone blocks `V`, since
 186: `V` later wins.  Thus `V` has deficits `4,3,2,1` at `q_1,q_2,q_3,q_4`.
 187: The total-count lower bound (75.1) matches each value, proving that these are
 188: the exact shadow minima.
 189: 
 190: At `q_1,q_2,q_3`, (74.1) would require `mu_R<=2`, contrary to the displayed
 191: lower bounds.  At `q_4` it would require `mu_R=1`, again contrary to the
 192: lower bound.  The real append associated with `z_3` lowers any real-window
 193: deficit by at most one, which also proves (75.2).  S55 and S58 give the two
 194: stated slices. QED.
 195: 
 196: The theorem locates two different facts which must not be conflated.  The
 197: fast line fails `RES_1` at every `q_j` and is **never admitted** to `R_1` at
 198: either eligible `FirstStone` entrance `q_1` or `q_3`, so there is no honest
 199: in-class "ejection event."  The earliest failed entrance is `q_1`.  The
 200: sharp terminal-proximate readiness failure is the `q_3 -> q_4` event in
 201: (75.2).  Calling only the latter an ejection would falsely imply earlier
 202: reserve membership; `q_2` and `q_4` are readiness checks, not rolling-cycle
 203: entrances.
 204: 
 205: Even the best real geometry on the `mu_R=3` slice is one event late.  If the
 206: two real appends both hit one deficit-three window, the paired minima along
 207: the terminal turn can evolve
 208: 
 209: `(2,3) -> (1,2) -> (0,1)`                                  (75.3)
 210: 
 211: in shadow/real order.  The last state has a shadow win and only a real
 212: deficit-one window.  That is a prepaid threat for a hypothetical later F
 213: move, not a same-event real terminal certificate; the real turn has ended
 214: and the shadow game has stopped.
